.SH COMPLETION
.B zish
provides context-aware tab completion with ghost text prediction.
.SS Completion Types
.IP \(bu 2
Commands (builtins, aliases, executables in PATH)
.IP \(bu 2
File and directory paths (with trailing slash for directories)
.IP \(bu 2
Variables ($VAR)
.IP \(bu 2
Git branches (local, remote, packed-refs, tags, stash entries)
.IP \(bu 2
Git subcommands
.IP \(bu 2
Docker containers and images
.IP \(bu 2
Make targets (from Makefile)
.IP \(bu 2
Just recipes (from justfile)
.IP \(bu 2
Systemctl units
.IP \(bu 2
SSH hosts (from ~/.ssh/config)
.IP \(bu 2
Process IDs and signal names (for kill)
.IP \(bu 2
Zig build targets
.IP \(bu 2
Cargo binaries
.IP \(bu 2
npm scripts
.IP \(bu 2
Command flags with descriptions (parsed from \-\-help output)
.IP \(bu 2
Pipe targets (common commands after |)
.IP \(bu 2
History-based prefix matching
.SS Ghost Text
When a local GGUF model is configured (see \fBcompletion_model\fR in
.IR CONFIGURATION ),
zish displays predictive ghost text in dim color ahead of the cursor.
Press
.B Right
or
.B End
to accept the full prediction, or
.B Ctrl\-Right
to accept one word at a time.
Ghost text is generated asynchronously on a background thread
using pure-Zig inference with no external dependencies.
.SH SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING
.B zish
highlights input as you type using ANSI 256-color codes:
.IP \(bu 2
Builtins and keywords in distinct colors
.IP \(bu 2
Valid commands in green, invalid in red
.IP \(bu 2
Strings and quoted text highlighted
.IP \(bu 2
Variables and expansions highlighted
.IP \(bu 2
Operators and redirections highlighted
.SH PROMPT
The default prompt format is:
.PP
.RS
.B [M] user@host path (branch) [exit] $
.RE
.PP
.TP
.B [M]
Vi mode indicator:
.B [N]
(blue) normal,
.B [I]
(cyan) insert,
.B [V]
(yellow) visual,
.B [VL]
(yellow) visual line
.TP
.B user@host
Green. Current username and hostname.
.TP
.B path
Cyan. Working directory with ~ expansion for home.
.TP
.B (branch)
Yellow. Current git branch, shown only inside git repositories.
.TP
.B [exit]
Red. Last exit code, shown only when non-zero.
.SH COMMAND SUGGESTIONS
When a command is not found, zish suggests similar commands
using Levenshtein distance matching against builtins, aliases,
and executables in PATH.
.SH JOB CONTROL
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Z
Suspend foreground job
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-C
Interrupt foreground job
.PP
Job specifications: %1 (job 1), %+ (current), %\- (previous), %string (match).
.SH HISTORY
Command history is saved to \fB~/.zish_history\fR.
.PP
History expansion:
.TP
.B !!
Previous command
.TP
.B !n
Command number n
.TP
.B !string
Most recent command starting with string
.SH AGENT
The
.B agent
builtin provides an embedded LLM agent with tool use,
recursive subagent spawning, and autonomous task execution.
The agent runs on a dedicated background thread and communicates
with the shell via a lock-free SPSC ring buffer.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B agent
Enter interactive agent mode. Displays model name in header.
.TP
.B agent c\fR, \fBagent continue
Re-enter interactive mode, continuing the current session.
.TP
.B agent attach \fR[\fIid\fR]
Attach to a running session via FIFO pipe (like screen/tmux).
Input is sent through \fB~/.zish/sessions/\fIid\fB/ctl\fR.
.TP
.B agent \fIquery\fR
Send a one-shot query and print the response.
.TP
.B agent \-m \fImodel\fR \fR[\fIquery\fR]
Switch model before query. Accepts aliases:
.BR opus ", " sonnet ", " haiku ,
or a full model ID string.
.TP
.B agent stop
Cancel the current agent operation.
.TP
.B agent status
Show whether the agent is busy or idle.
.TP
.B agent sessions
List recent sessions (last 10).
.TP
.B agent log \fR[\fIid\fR]
View conversation log with markdown rendering.
Shows latest session if no id given.
.TP
.B agent tasks
List subagent tasks from the current session.
.TP
.B agent config
Show current agent configuration.
.TP
.B agent clear
Reset conversation history and start fresh.
.TP
.B agent help
Show agent usage information.
.SS Interactive Mode
In interactive mode, the agent presents a bottom-anchored TUI
using ANSI scroll regions. Output stays in terminal scrollback
(no alternate screen). The layout consists of:
.IP \(bu 2
Output scroll region (top rows)
.IP \(bu 2
Separator line with scroll indicator
.IP \(bu 2
Multiline input area (grows up to 5 lines)
.IP \(bu 2
Status bar: model name, session cost, elapsed time, keybinding hints
.PP
The input area uses
.B \(rA
as the prompt character.
Press
.B Enter
to submit,
.B Ctrl\-J
or
.B Alt\-Enter
to insert a newline.
.B PageUp / PageDown
scrolls through message history (512KB ring buffer).
.SS Interactive Commands
.TP
.B /compact
Summarize conversation to save context window space.
.TP
.B /cost
Show session cost breakdown.
.TP
.B /model \fR[\fIname\fR]
Show or switch model (opus, sonnet, haiku, or full model ID).
.TP
.B /diff
Show uncommitted git changes.
.TP
.B /commit \fR[\fImsg\fR]
Commit changes. If no message given, the agent generates one.
.TP
.B /review
AI code review of all changes made this session.
.TP
.B /undo
Undo the last file change made by the agent.
.TP
.B /plan \fItask\fR
Plan a task without executing (read-only analysis).
.TP
.B /spawn \fItask\fR
Spawn a full-tools worker subagent for parallel work.
.TP
.B /queue \fItask\fR
Queue an autonomous task for execution when agent is idle.
.TP
.B /agents\fR, \fB/tasks
Show subagent workers and task queue status.
.TP
.B /sessions
List recent sessions.
.TP
.B /config
Show current configuration.
.TP
.B /search \fIpattern\fR\fR, \fB/s \fIpattern\fR
Search message history backwards (case-insensitive, strips ANSI).
.TP
.B /help
Show available interactive commands.
.TP
.B clear
Reset conversation history.
.TP
.B exit\fR, \fBquit\fR, \fBCtrl\-D
Leave agent mode.
.PP
Additional prefixes:
.TP
.B !\fIcommand\fR
Run a shell command directly via the zish engine.
.TP
.B !
Drop to a full zish shell prompt.
.TP
.B ?\fIintent\fR
Translate natural language to a shell command.
.SS Agent Tools
The agent has access to the following built-in tools:
.TP
.B Bash
Execute shell commands. Supports git, builds, package managers.
.TP
.B Read
Read file contents with line numbers. Supports
.I offset
and
.I limit
parameters for reading sections of large files.
.TP
.B Edit
Make exact string replacements in files (find and replace).
Shows a diff preview of changes. Requires reading the file first.
.TP
.B Write
Create a new file or overwrite an existing file.
.TP
.B Glob
Find files matching a glob pattern (e.g., "**/*.zig").
.TP
.B Grep
Search file contents using regular expressions.
.TP
.B Agent
Spawn a recursive subagent for autonomous parallel tasks.
Subagents can themselves spawn further subagents (see
.IR "RECURSIVE SUBAGENTS" ).
.TP
.B WebFetch
Fetch a URL and convert HTML to plain text.
.TP
.B WebSearch
Search the web using DuckDuckGo.
.PP
Tool calls are displayed as
.B "\(bu ToolName(args)"
during execution, with results shown after completion.
.SS Plugin Tools
Custom tools can be defined in \fB~/.zish/agent-tools.json\fR (max 16).
Format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[{
  "name": "deploy",
  "description": "Deploy the application",
  "command": "deploy.sh {environment}",
  "parameters": { "environment": "target env" },
  "confirm": true
}]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Parameters use \fB{param}\fR substitution. All parameter values are
shell-escaped with single-quote wrapping.
Tools with \fB"confirm": true\fR require user approval before execution.
.SS Recursive Subagents
The agent can spawn subagents up to 3 levels deep,
with a maximum of 8 concurrent subagents per level.
Worker subagents inherit the full tool set from their parent,
including the ability to spawn their own subagents.
Read-only subagents receive only Bash, Read, Glob, and Grep.
Each subagent makes its own API calls and has atomic status tracking.
The parent agent blocks on each subagent result with a 120-second timeout.
.SS Autonomous Tasks
The task queue holds up to 16 autonomous tasks.
Tasks are submitted via \fB/queue\fR and execute during the agent's
idle exhale phase. Use \fB/tasks\fR to inspect queue status.
.SS Auto-Compact
When total input tokens exceed 120K and history contains more than
6 messages, the agent automatically summarizes the conversation
to free context window space. Old messages are dropped and replaced
with a summary, preserving the last 4 messages. The token counter
resets after compaction. Manual compaction is available via \fB/compact\fR.
The visible TUI history (ring buffer) is not affected.
.SS Token Tracking
The agent parses SSE events (message_start, message_delta) to track
input and output tokens. Cumulative session cost is displayed in the
status bar and available via \fB/cost\fR.
.SS CLAUDE.md
The agent automatically loads CLAUDE.md files from the current
working directory, walking up parent directories and checking
\fB.claude/CLAUDE.md\fR as well. Contents are prepended to the
system prompt (up to 3KB, within an 8KB system prompt buffer).
.SS Confirmation Flow
When a tool call requires confirmation, the agent pushes a
.B confirm_request
message. The main thread displays the request, reads a keystroke
from the user, and pushes a
.B confirm_response
back to the agent thread.
.SH QUERY ROUTER
When enabled, the query router classifies user input before
dispatching to the agent, reducing cost by routing simple
queries to cheaper models or bypassing the LLM entirely.
.SS Classification Pipeline
.IP 1. 4
Local pattern matching classifies approximately 70% of queries
at zero cost, detecting shell commands and keyword patterns.
.IP 2. 4
Local GGUF inference (if configured) provides model-based
classification using pure-Zig inference with no external dependencies.
.IP 3. 4
API call to Haiku (~300ms, ~300 tokens) for ambiguous queries.
.IP 4. 4
Default to Sonnet if all classifiers are unavailable.
.SS Route Decisions
Each classification produces a structured RouteDecision:
.TP
.B action
.BR shell " (bypass LLM entirely),"
.BR agent " (send to LLM),"
.BR fan_out " (parallel subagent dispatch)"
.TP
.B model tier
.BR haiku " (fast/cheap),"
.BR sonnet " (balanced),"
.BR opus " (reasoning-heavy)"
.TP
.B tool mask
Subset of tools the agent may use for this query.
.PP
The router never talks to the user directly; routing decisions
are displayed as dim
.B [route info]
annotations.
.SS Shell Action
When the router detects a direct shell command (e.g., \fBls\fR,
\fBgit status\fR), it runs the command via \fB/bin/sh \-c\fR
and bypasses the LLM entirely.
.SH LOCAL INFERENCE
.B zish
includes a pure-Zig GGUF inference engine with no external
dependencies. It supports Transformer models with RoPE,
RMSNorm, SwiGLU MLP, grouped-query attention, and KV cache.
.SS Process Isolation
The inference engine uses fork-based process isolation.
The parent process communicates with the child via pipes.
The child process loads the model via mmap and runs continuous
inference, self-feeding tokens between requests to keep
internal state warm.
.SS Quantization
Supported formats: F32, F16, Q8_0 (SIMD matmul), Q4_K
(fused dot product), Q6_K (fused dot product). Fused dot
products operate directly on quantized weights without
intermediate f32 buffers.
.SS Multi-threaded Matmul
Matrix multiplications are split across available CPU cores
(up to 8 threads), with the last chunk executed on the
calling thread.
.SS Sliding Window Attention
Models with sliding window metadata (e.g., Mistral) use
windowed attention, reducing complexity from O(T\(sq) to O(T\(muW).
.SS CTM (Continuous Thinking Model)
CTM support adds iterative thinking blocks on top of the
standard MLP. CTM is additive: the MLP always runs first,
and CTM adds thinking as a second residual. CTM blocks
include SuperLinear layers, SynapseUNET, and dual
synchronization accumulators.
.PP
CTM parameters are read from GGUF metadata:
.BR ctm.enabled ", " ctm.iterations ", "
.BR ctm.memory_length ", " ctm.n_synch .
.SS CTM Plasticity
The inference engine supports online learning through
a breathing architecture:
.TP
.B Dopamine gating
Surprise-based neuromodulation, clamped [0.5, 1.0],
prevents positive feedback loops.
.TP
.B Hebbian learning
Gradient-free weight updates from synchronization patterns
with novelty gating and homeostasis (1% norm cap).
.TP
.B Replay buffer
64-entry ring buffer of token sequences with surprise scores.
Persisted to a \fB.replay\fR file alongside the model.
.TP
.B Breathing loop
Inhale (dopamine-tracked generation) and exhale (compact
every 100 steps, save every 1000 steps).
.PP
State files are stored as siblings of the GGUF model file:
\fB.ctmstate\fR (sync accumulators),
\fB.plastic\fR (weight overlay),
\fB.replay\fR (replay buffer).
.SH SESSION MANAGEMENT
Agent sessions are logged in JSONL format under
\fB~/.zish/sessions/\fIid\fB/conversation.jsonl\fR.
.PP
Sessions can be listed with \fBagent sessions\fR, viewed with
\fBagent log\fR, and attached to (like screen/tmux) with
\fBagent attach\fR via a FIFO at
\fB~/.zish/sessions/\fIid\fB/ctl\fR.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.SS Agent Configuration
.B ~/.zish/agent.json
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "provider": "anthropic",
  "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  "base_url": "https://api.anthropic.com",
  "api_key": "sk-...",
  "api_key_cmd": "pass show anthropic",
  "max_tokens": 8192,
  "max_tool_iterations": 10,
  "max_agents": 8,
  "auto_allow": false,
  "router_enabled": false,
  "router_provider": "anthropic",
  "router_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
  "router_base_url": "https://api.anthropic.com",
  "router_local_only": false,
  "router_local_model": "/path/to/router.gguf",
  "completion_model": "/path/to/completion.gguf",
  "haiku_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
  "sonnet_model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
  "opus_model": "claude-opus-4-6"
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B provider
API provider: "anthropic", "ollama", or "openai".
.TP
.B model
Default model ID.
.TP
.B base_url
API endpoint URL.
.TP
.B api_key
API key. Overridden by ZISH_AGENT_KEY or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY environment variables.
.TP
.B api_key_cmd
Shell command to retrieve API key dynamically.
.TP
.B max_tokens
Maximum tokens per API response (default: 8192).
.TP
.B max_tool_iterations
Maximum tool calls per query (default: 10).
.TP
.B max_agents
Maximum concurrent subagents (default: 8).
.TP
.B auto_allow
Auto-approve all tool calls without confirmation (default: false).
.TP
.B router_enabled
Enable the query router (default: false).
.TP
.B router_local_only
Never call router API; use local pattern matching only.
.TP
.B router_local_model
Path to a local GGUF model for router inference.
.TP
.B completion_model
Path to a local GGUF model for ghost text prediction.
.TP
.B haiku_model\fR, \fBsonnet_model\fR, \fBopus_model
Model IDs for each tier, used by the router.
.SS Authentication
If no API key is configured, zish auto-loads Claude Code
OAuth credentials from \fB~/.claude/.credentials.json\fR
and sends appropriate identification headers.
.SS Plugin Tools
.B ~/.zish/agent-tools.json
.PP
JSON array of custom tool definitions. See
.IR "AGENT TOOLS" .
.SS Keybindings
.B ~/.zish/keybindings.json
.PP
Custom keybinding overrides loaded at shell startup.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Home directory for cd and tilde expansion.
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search for commands.
.TP
.B PS1
Primary prompt string (supports escape sequences).
.TP
.B OLDPWD
Previous working directory (for cd \-).
.TP
.B PWD
Current working directory.
.TP
.B ZISH_AGENT_KEY
API key for the agent (takes precedence over config file).
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
Anthropic API key (fallback if ZISH_AGENT_KEY is not set).
.TP
.B ?
Exit status of last command.
.TP
.B $
Process ID of shell.
.TP
.B !
Process ID of last background command.
.TP
.B #
Number of positional parameters.
.TP
.B @
All positional parameters as separate words.
.TP
.B *
All positional parameters as single word.
.TP
.B 0
Name of shell or script.
.TP
.B 1, 2, ...
Positional parameters.
.SH PROMPT ESCAPES
The PS1 variable supports these escape sequences:
.TP
.B \eu
Username
.TP
.B \eh
Hostname (short)
.TP
.B \eH
Hostname (full)
.TP
.B \ew
Working directory (~ for home)
.TP
.B \eW
Basename of working directory
.TP
.B \e$
# if root, $ otherwise
.TP
.B \en
Newline
.TP
.B \et
Time (HH:MM:SS)
.TP
.B \ed
Date
.TP
.B \ee
Escape character
.TP
.B \e[, \e]
Begin/end non-printing sequence
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.zishrc
Startup file for interactive shells.
.TP
.B ~/.zish_history
Command history file.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/agent.json
Agent configuration file.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/agent-tools.json
Custom agent tool definitions.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/keybindings.json
Custom keybinding overrides.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/sessions/
Agent session logs in JSONL format.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/sessions/\fIid\fB/ctl
FIFO pipe for session attachment.
.TP
.B ~/.zish/models/
Local GGUF models for inference.
.TP
.B ~/.claude/.credentials.json
Claude Code OAuth credentials (auto-loaded).
.TP
.B CLAUDE.md, .claude/CLAUDE.md
Agent system prompt context (loaded from cwd and parents).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is that of the last command executed,
or 0 if no command was executed.
With \fB\-c\fR, the exit status is that of the command string.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a command:
.RS
.nf
zish \-c 'echo hello'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Benchmark a command with histogram:
.RS
.nf
time \-n 100 \-w 5 \-H ./myprogram
.fi
.RE
.PP
One-shot agent query:
.RS
.nf
agent "explain this error" 2>&1 | head
.fi
.RE
.PP
Agent with model override:
.RS
.nf
agent \-m opus "review this function for security issues"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Interactive agent session:
.RS
.nf
agent
> /spawn refactor the auth module
> /spawn write tests for the API
> /tasks
.fi
.RE
.PP
Process substitution:
.RS
.nf
paste <(cut \-f1 file1) <(cut \-f2 file2)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Array operations:
.RS
.nf
files=(*.txt)
for f in "${files[@]}"; do
    echo "Processing $f"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Natural language to command:
.RS
.nf
?find all rust files larger than 1MB
.fi
.RE
